WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:06.460
I think we've got to see that a riot is the
language of the unheard.

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:11.030
To justify the violent tactics of some of
the protesters in the demonstrations that

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:16.000
have swept the country over the past week,
Martin Luther King Jr.'s famous line has been

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.640
circulating once again.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:21.460
Last Saturday, King's own son posted it to
Twitter.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:26.620
But that quote has been widely misinterpreted
because it's taken out of context.

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:34.350
Nonviolence is the most potent weapon available
to the Negro in his struggle for freedom and justice.

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:41.960
I think for the Negro to turn to violence
would be both impractical and immoral.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:48.440
King sought to make his position crystal clear
in his 1996 interview with 60 Minutes' Mike Wallace.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:54.860
I would hope that we can avoid riots because
riots are self defeating, and socially destructive.

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:59.890
King considered violent protests to be self
defeating because as he argued in 1968, every

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:06.920
time a riot develops it makes a right wing take over more likely, and helps the segregationist George Wallace.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:12.700
The violent clashes over the last week have
already created a backlash with 58% of respondents

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:16.680
to a recent poll saying they support the use
of military force.

00:01:16.689 --> 00:01:21.340
Please, show me where it says that protesters
are supposed to be polite and peaceful.

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:26.390
Journalists such as CNN's Chris Cuomo and
recent Pulizter Prize Winner Nikole Hannah-Jones

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:31.080
have downplayed the seriousness of property destruction and violence by some protesters.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:38.940
Violence is when an agent of the state kneels on a man's neck until all of the life leaves out of his body.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:44.060
Destroying property which can be replaced is not violent.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:48.580
But political leaders in some of the communities where the property destruction has taken place,

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:54.280
have shown that it's possible both to acknowledge the horrors of police brutality while at the same time

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:57.320
denouncing theft, violence, and destruction.

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:01.260
These people did this for no reason.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:08.060
It's not going to bring George back here, George is in a better place than we are.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:14.060
This is not in the spirit of Martin Luther King Jr., this is chaos.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:21.820
A protest has purpose. When Doctor King was assasinated we didn't do this to our city.

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:28.020
Like King, the best political and civil rights leaders of the 1960's were able both to condemn rioting,

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:34.360
and to acknowledge the legitimacy of the greivances that brought protesters into the streets in the first place.

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:39.760
New York Mayor John Lindsey soothed racial tensions following King's 1968 assassination

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:44.560
by calmly walking the streets of Harlem with other civil rights leaders and reminding the public

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:47.580
of the fallen leaders' unwavering opposition to violence.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.560
The city's current mayor, Bill de Blasio,

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.940
has lacked the courage to forcefully condemn both rioting and vandalism,

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:56.340
and the violent tactics of his own police force.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:01.200
Minnesota official's initially deflected blame for the riots onto outside infiltrators

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:04.160
before the press exposed their misinformation.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:09.740
And President Trump hid in a bunker and tweeted, out incendiary messages about the coming show of force

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:10.920
against the rioters.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:19.340
If a city or state refuses to take the actions that are necessary to defend life and property of their residents,

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:26.560
then I will deploy the United States military and quickly solve the problem for them.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:31.940
Politicians, like Atlanta Mayor Keisha Bottoms, have said the ultimate answer lies at the ballot box.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:37.300
If you want change in America, go and and register to vote. Do it in November.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:41.820
While voting for candidates who support policing and criminal justice reform could have

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:46.740
marginal impact in the long run, protestors are demanding immediate action.

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:50.860
When police harass and assault protesters, they should be held accountable.

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:52.620
Through anti-choke hold bills,

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:57.160
and by putting an ends to the legal doctrine that protects them from criminal prosecution.

00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:04.060
The internet has further decentralized activism, which has made today's protest less uniform than ever before.

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:09.100
In the final analysis, a riot is the language of the unheard.

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:14.940
When King said this, he wasn't excusing riots, but imploring those watching them unfold in horror

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:21.120
to acknowledge the unjust conditions that contributed to their emergence in the first place.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:32.520
Certain conditions continue to exist in our society, which must be condemned as vigorously as we condemn riots.

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:39.920
With no Martin Luther King Jr. of our own, we all bear the burden moreso of condemning the initiation of violence,

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:42.860
no matter the perpetrator, no matter the cause.

